@@ -0,0 +1,174 @@ # Declarative Jellyfin + OIDC mode
+# Declarative Jellyfin + OIDC mode
+
+Set `SEERR_CONFIG_FILE` to an operator-owned JSON file and restart Seerr. This
+mode is deliberately opinionated: Jellyfin, OIDC-only authentication, and
+permissions supplied by every OIDC provider. The configuration file is the
+source of truth, not a seed for mutable settings.
+
+## Example
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "owner": {
+    "provider": "authentik",
+    "subject": "the-exact-oidc-sub-of-the-owner",
+    "email": "owner@example.com",
+    "username": "owner"
+  },
+  "settings": {
+    "main": {
+      "applicationUrl": "https://requests.example.com",
+      "applicationTitle": "Requests",
+      "locale": "pt-BR"
+    },
+    "oidc": {
+      "providers": [
+        {
+          "slug": "authentik",
+          "name": "SSO",
+          "issuerUrl": "https://auth.example.com/application/o/seerr/",
+          "clientId": "seerr",
+          "clientSecret": { "file": "/run/credentials/seerr.service/oidc" },
+          "scopes": "openid profile email seerr_permissions",
+          "permissionsClaim": "seerr_permissions",
+          "newUserLogin": true
+        }
+      ]
+    },
+    "jellyfin": {
+      "name": "Jellyfin",
+      "ip": "localhost",
+      "port": 8096,
+      "serverId": "your-jellyfin-server-id",
+      "apiKey": { "file": "/run/credentials/seerr.service/jellyfin" },
+      "externalHostname": "https://jellyfin.example.com",
+      "libraries": [
+        { "id": "your-movies-library-id", "name": "Movies", "enabled": true, "type": "movie" },
+        { "id": "your-tv-library-id", "name": "TV", "enabled": true, "type": "show" }
+      ]
+    },
+    "radarr": [
+      {
+        "id": 1,
+        "name": "Radarr",
+        "hostname": "localhost",
+        "port": 7878,
+        "apiKey": { "file": "/run/credentials/seerr.service/radarr" },
+        "activeProfileId": 1,
+        "activeProfileName": "HD",
+        "activeDirectory": "/media/movies",
+        "isDefault": true
+      }
+    ],
+    "sonarr": [
+      {
+        "id": 1,
+        "name": "Sonarr",
+        "hostname": "localhost",
+        "port": 8989,
+        "apiKey": { "file": "/run/credentials/seerr.service/sonarr" },
+        "activeProfileId": 1,
+        "activeProfileName": "HD",
+        "activeDirectory": "/media/tv",
+        "isDefault": true
+      }
+    ]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Secret paths may be absolute or relative to the configuration file. Exactly one
+trailing LF (or CRLF) is removed. Missing/empty secret files fail startup. Inline
+secret strings are supported but should not be put in a public repository or
+Nix store. Configuration errors never echo supplied input values.
+
+Connection IDs, library IDs, quality-profile IDs and directory paths are explicit;
+no first-match discovery or accidental reassignment of existing requests. Arrays
+replace defaults, and duplicate IDs or multiple defaults for one resolution are
+rejected. Both normal and 4K connections are supported. Sonarr's anime overrides
+and Radarr's minimum-availability setting use their existing setting names.
+
+The accepted keys are defined in `server/lib/settings/declarative.ts`. Unknown
+keys and wrong types fail startup. Network and metadata preferences can also be
+configured. `settings.notifications.agents` accepts the existing notification
+agent names and options; credential fields accept either strings or `{ "file":
+"/path/to/credential" }`. For example:
+
+```json
+{
+  "notifications": {
+    "agents": {
+      "email": {
+        "enabled": true,
+        "options": {
+          "smtpHost": "mail.example.com",
+          "smtpPort": 465,
+          "secure": true,
+          "authUser": "requests@example.com",
+          "authPass": { "file": "/run/credentials/seerr.service/smtp" }
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  },
+  "jobs": {
+    "radarr-scan": { "schedule": "0 15 4 * * *" }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+These keys go inside `settings`. Job names and cron schedules are validated;
+omitted jobs keep their defaults. Omitted notifications remain disabled. Plex
+and Emby are not supported in declarative mode.
+
+## Ownership and bootstrap
+
+The owner is **not** selected by first login or by email matching. On an empty
+database startup creates user 1, with administrator permissions, and links the
+specified `(provider, subject)` identity. On an existing database the operator's
+configuration explicitly provisions that same user 1. If that identity already
+belongs to another user, or user 1 has another subject at that provider, startup
+fails transactionally. Changing a pinned identity requires an explicit database
+migration, not an edit that silently transfers ownership.
+
+Supply the actual OIDC `sub`, not a username or email address. The provider slug
+and issuer must stay stable. The owner should receive `ADMIN` from the IdP:
+permissions are synchronized from claims at login, including for the owner.
+
+The setup wizard is bypassed. Configuration mutation APIs and account-linking
+mutation APIs return 403, including requests from administrators. The server
+settings screens show a configuration-managed notice instead of editable forms.
+User preferences and request management remain available. Operational settings
+POST endpoints (including manual job triggers) are also disabled for now.
+
+## State and migration
+
+The database remains writable and persistent. Requests, users, identities,
+preferences, media metadata and sessions are not configuration.
+
+`CONFIG_DIRECTORY/declarative-state.json` stores only generated application
+identity: client ID, session secret, VAPID keys and Seerr's API key. Writes are
+atomic and mode 0600. Connection credentials are resolved in memory and are not
+written to this file. Configuration is loaded after defaults; legacy connection
+settings cannot override it.
+
+When first enabling this mode, existing generated identity is imported from
+`settings.json` if no declarative state exists, preserving sessions and application
+identity. The legacy file is left untouched; it may contain old connection
+credentials. Back it up securely and remove it after migration if desired. A
+corrupt state file is an error, not a reason to generate a new session secret.
+Disabling declarative mode returns to the legacy settings file; this is not an
+automatic reverse migration.
+
+## Authorization limitations
+
+Permission names are validated against Seerr's permission enum. Invalid/missing
+claims reject login; an empty array grants no permissions. Alternate login paths
+are disabled when permissions are managed.
+
+This is login-time synchronization, **not continuous IdP authorization**. Removing
+an IdP group or disabling an IdP account does not revoke an existing Seerr
+session. Access-token expiration at the IdP does not bound Seerr sessions. Session
+revalidation/revocation is a separate concern and must be implemented before
+claiming immediate IdP-driven revocation. The application API key remains a
+trusted administrative credential.
